    "Trait": {
     "choice": "literature",
     "probs": {
      "measured": 0.002,
      "literature": 0.998
     },
     "said": [
      "## Life-history traits Carabid beetles and spiders were assigned to lifehistory trait categories with the help of literature data and using morpho-physiological and phenological features which potentially affect the occurrence patterns of these species (Ribera et al.",
      "FDis is a measure of functional richness, which considers species relative abundances by estimating their dispersion in a multidimensional trait space (Laliberté & Legendre 2010)."
     ],
     "value": "literature"
    },
    "Category": {
     "choice": "literature",
     "probs": {
      "measured": 0.005,
      "literature": 0.995
     },
     "said": [
      "## Life-history traits To detect possible differences in life-history trait composition among successional stages, we applied GLMs with the number of species of a trait category per plot as the dependent variable (e.g., number of very small spider species per plot)."
     ],
     "value": "literature"
    }
   }
  },
  "1": {
   "long": false,
   "traits": {
    "Successional stages G": {
     "choice": "measured",
     "probs": {
      "measured": 0.684,
      "literature": 0.316
     },
     "said": [
      "Five main stages of (coastal) heathland succession can be found (Remke 2003): (i) open acidic (coastal) dunes (grey dunes) dominated by Corynephorus canescens, Carex arenaria and cryptogams representing the youngest successional stage (G); (ii) dwarf-shrub heath dominated by Calluna vulgaris and Empetrum nigrum, the main vegetation type within the study area (H); (iii) grassy heath, where the majority of the dwarf-shrubs are displaced by tall graminoids, especially by C.",
      "The five successional stages differed in habitat heterogeneity (expressed as the Simpson-Diversity of estimated cover of ‘trees’, ‘shrubs’, ‘field layer’, ‘C."
     ],
     "value": null
    },
